            "content": "{\"found\":true,\"nodeId\":\"n_1790200000274_102169806\",\"type\":\"prompt\",\"title\":\"Extract kinase-substrate pairs\",\"contentLock\":{\"locked\":true,\"usedBy\":[{\"nodeId\":\"n_1790200000411_103254709\",\"type\":\"run\",\"title\":\"Generate\"},{\"nodeId\":\"n_1791936940897_884411178\",\"type\":\"run\",\"title\":\"Generate\"},{\"nodeId\":\"n_1792099294390_944995513\",\"type\":\"run\",\"title\":\"Generate\"}],\"editableFields\":[\"name\",\"note\"]},\"upstream\":[],\"downstream\":[{\"connection\":\"prompt-to-prompt\",\"nodeId\":\"n_1792023944229_932459535\",\"type\":\"prompt\",\"title\":\"Extract kinase-substrate pairs v2\"},{\"connection\":\"prompt-to-run\",\"nodeId\":\"n_1792099294390_944995513\",\"type\":\"run\",\"title\":\"Generate\"},{\"connection\":\"prompt-to-run\",\"nodeId\":\"n_1791936940897_884411178\",\"type\":\"run\",\"title\":\"Generate\"},{\"connection\":\"prompt-to-run\",\"nodeId\":\"n_1790200000411_103254709\",\"type\":\"run\",\"title\":\"Generate\"}],\"ports\":{\"inputs\":[],\"outputs\":[{\"id\":\"prompt\",\"readBy\":[{\"nodeId\":\"n_1792023944229_932459535\",\"lineage\":true},{\"nodeId\":\"n_1792099294390_944995513\",\"portId\":\"prompt\"},{\"nodeId\":\"n_1791936940897_884411178\",\"portId\":\"prompt\"},{\"nodeId\":\"n_1790200000411_103254709\",\"portId\":\"prompt\"}]}]},\"details\":{\"note\":\"kin_prompt/extract_pair.py\",\"text\":\"You are a bioinformatics expert. Your job is to determine whether this paper provides direct biochemical evidence that a protein kinase phosphorylates a protein substrate.\\n\\nWe retrieved the article titled **{title}** from PubMed and located the following passage:\\n\\n{excerpt}\\n\\nBased on the title and passage, you already have a general idea of the study. Your goal is to act as a human biologist and identify the most significant and well-supported kinase-substrate pairs.\\n\\nPlease inspect the raw text and extract only the most significant kinase-substrate relationships as follows:\\n\\nIdentify the Primary Findings: Focus on the main conclusions of the paper.\\n\\nEXCLUSION RULES (Very Strict - Apply Before Extracting Anything)\\n\\n1. Pathway-level / phenotypic evidence -> EXCLUDE\\nDo NOT extract if the relationship is mentioned only in signaling cascades, pathway diagrams, physiological effects, or general pathway modulation (e.g., \\\"ERK pathway\\\", \\\"Akt regulates mTOR signaling\\\", \\\"JNK activates apoptosis\\\").\\nIf substrate phosphorylation is not directly measured AND the text does not explicitly state a kinase-mediated phosphorylation event -> exclude, EXCEPT when:\\n- a specific phosphosite is explicitly mentioned, AND\\n- the site is linked to a named kinase or kinase family (e.g., PKA site, Akt site), AND\\n- functional or mutational evidence (e.g., phospho-mimetic mutation, kinase activation/inhibition) supports phosphorylation at that site.\\nDO NOT exclude if the text explicitly states kinase-mediated phosphorylation of a specific substrate, or explicitly describes a residue as a phosphorylation site or autophosphorylation site of a named kinase/substrate pair.\\n\\n2. Inhibitor-only evidence -> EXCLUDE\\nEvidence based solely on kinase inhibitors (e.g., LY294002, Wortmannin, U0126, SB203580, SP600125, H89) does NOT qualify unless phosphorylation of the substrate is directly measured OR supports phosphorylation-dependent functional effects at a defined site.\\n\\n3. Reagent kinases -> EXCLUDE\\nIgnore kinases used only as experimental tools (e.g., recombinant PKA, PKC, ERK purchased as reagents) unless the specific human kinase (PRKACA, PRKCA, MAPK1, etc.) is named as the enzyme responsible for phosphorylation.\\n\\n4. Non-protein kinases or non-protein substrates -> EXCLUDE\\nExclude lipid kinases (PI3K acting on PIP2/PIP3), carbohydrate kinases, and nucleotide kinases (AMPK acting on AMP, etc.). Only protein -> protein phosphorylation pairs are allowed.\\n\\n5. Fusion protein rule\\nIf a fusion protein (e.g., BCR-ABL, EML4-ALK) is mentioned, map the substrate to the native UniProt protein (ABL1, ALK) and extract only if direct biochemical phosphorylation is shown. If mapping is unclear -> exclude.\\n\\n6. Contradictory evidence -> EXCLUDE\\nExclude pairs if the text states \\\"no phosphorylation\\\", \\\"did not phosphorylate\\\", \\\"unchanged phosphorylation\\\", \\\"did not bind\\\", \\\"no effect\\\", or any explicit negation of kinase activity.\\n\\n7. Generic assay substrate -> EXCLUDE\\nExclude substrates used only as generic kinase assay readouts or external substrates (e.g., myelin basic protein (MBP), casein, histone H1, enolase, synthetic peptides), especially when described as \\\"external substrate\\\", \\\"exogenous substrate\\\", or \\\"used to measure kinase activity\\\".\\n\\nTIERED EVIDENCE (What to Extract)\\n\\nTier 1 - Direct biochemical evidence (extract). Valid evidence includes purified protein kinase assays, in vitro phosphorylation of substrate, direct substrate phosphorylation measurement (p-Ab, MS, autoradiography), kinase overexpression increasing substrate phosphorylation, kinase knockdown/KO decreasing phosphorylation, an identified phosphosite (Ser/Thr/Tyr residues), mass-spec phosphosite confirmation, a catalytic mutant proving phosphorylation, or an explicit statement of \\\"X-mediated phosphorylation of Y at residue Z\\\". When reporting a phosphosite, always use the three-letter amino acid format (Ser/Thr/Tyr), e.g., report Ser988 rather than S988.\\n\\nTier 2 - Strong indirect evidence (only if Tier 1 missing). Allowed ONLY if ALL are true: substrate phosphorylation is directly measured OR a specific phosphosite is defined together with kinase-dependent functional evidence; perturbing the kinase changes that phosphorylation or supports phosphorylation-dependent functional effects; the effect is specific and not pathway-level; both proteins are UniProt-encoded; and the evidence is biochemical, not purely transcriptional or phenotypic. If no site is given, set phosphosite = null.\\n\\n1. **Locate Evidence Sentences**\\nRead the raw text carefully and find the sentences (or fragments) that describe kinases and their substrates.\\n\\n2. **Identify Distinct Kinase-Substrate Pairs**\\nFrom those evidence sentences, extract each kinase and the substrate it phosphorylates. If the same kinase phosphorylates different sites on the same substrate, consolidate into a single pair listing all sites. If multiple kinases are mentioned together (e.g., \\\"Erk1/2\\\"), split into separate pairs.\\n\\n3. **Normalize Names**\\nUse standardized identifiers where possible (gene names, UniProt names).\\n\\n4. **Identify Phosphosite**\\nIf explicitly provided, extract the site (Ser/Thr/Tyr format); otherwise null.\\n\\n5. **Record Evidence**\\nCollect all supporting sentences.\\n\\n6. **Explain Reasoning**\\nBriefly explain why the relationship is valid (direct vs indirect evidence).\\n\\n7. **Organism**\\nDefault to \\\"human\\\" unless otherwise specified.\\n\\n8. **Output format**\\nReturn a valid JSON object matching the schema. Each pair must include reasoning, kinase, substrate, phosphosite (or null), organism, is_specifically_mentioned, evidence_texts, and confidence (0-1).\",\"inputs\":[{\"name\":\"title\",\"type\":\"text\"},{\"name\":\"excerpt\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"output\":{\"returnShape\":\"table\",\"fields\":[{\"key\":\"reasoning\",\"type\":\"str\",\"description\":\"Brief justification explaining why this kinase-substrate relationship is included and which evidence tier it satisfies (Tier 1 explicit site-level phosphorylation, or Tier 2 strong indirect evidence with phosphorylation-state change). Mention key cues and explain any consolidation across multiple sites. Also justify organism choice if multiple are mentioned or if defaulted.\",\"examples\":[\"Two sentences show MAPK1 phosphorylates RUNX2 at Ser301; both experiments were performed in human U2OS cells, so the organism is set to human.\"],\"allowNone\":false},{\"key\":\"kinase\",\"type\":\"str\",\"description\":\"Normalized gene symbol (preferred) or canonical protein name for the endogenous protein kinase that phosphorylates the substrate, evidenced by biochemical measurement (not inferred from inhibitors, pathways, or phenotype alone). Excludes reagent/tool kinases, lipid kinases, and any kinase only implicated via inhibitor treatment without direct phosphorylation measurement.\",\"examples\":[\"MAPK1\",\"AKT1\",\"CDK1\"],\"allowNone\":false},{\"key\":\"substrate\",\"type\":\"str\",\"description\":\"Normalized gene symbol (preferred) or canonical protein name for the endogenous protein substrate that is phosphorylated. Must be a protein-coding gene present in UniProt. Exclude reagent enzymes and non-protein targets such as lipids, nucleotides, sugars, RNA, or DNA.\",\"examples\":[\"RUNX2\",\"INSR\",\"CDC25\"],\"allowNone\":false},{\"key\":\"phosphosite\",\"type\":\"str\",\"description\":\"Specific phosphorylation residue on the substrate explicitly stated in the excerpt. MUST be a string: single site (e.g., 'Ser301'), multiple sites as a semicolon-separated string (e.g., 'Ser262; Ser396'), or null if not found. Never use an array. Required only for Tier 1 evidence.\",\"examples\":[\"Ser301\",\"Ser262; Ser396\"],\"allowNone\":false},{\"key\":\"organism\",\"type\":\"str\",\"description\":\"Single-string organism label most relevant to the evidence sentences for this pair (e.g., 'human', 'mouse', 'rat', 'chicken'). If multiple organisms are mentioned, choose the one tied to the experiments supporting the pair. If organism cannot be determined from the provided text, default to 'human'.\",\"examples\":[\"human\",\"mouse\"],\"allowNone\":false},{\"key\":\"is_specifically_mentioned\",\"type\":\"str\",\"description\":\"true if the kinase name is a specific protein (gene symbol, protein name, or accession, e.g., MAPK1, AKT1, CDK1). false if it is a family or group term (e.g., 'MAPK', 'CDKs', 'PKC', 'Akt') without specifying a particular member. If uncertain, prefer false.\",\"examples\":[\"true\",\"false\"],\"allowNone\":false},{\"key\":\"evidence_texts\",\"type\":\"list_str\",\"description\":\"List of verbatim sentences or minimal fragments from the provided title and passage that directly support the kinase-substrate relationship and its tier classification. Include every sentence used for support. Do not include unrelated pathway background.\",\"examples\":[\"Western-blot analysis revealed that MAPK1 phosphorylated RUNX2 at Ser301 in human U2OS cells.\"],\"allowNone\":false},{\"key\":\"confidence\",\"type\":\"float\",\"description\":\"Your confidence in this extraction (0-1): 1=very certain from explicit evidence, 0.5=moderate inference, 0=uncertain.\",\"examples\":[\"0.95\",\"0.85\"],\"allowNone\":false}]}},\"diagnostics\":[]}",
