ates p53 at multiple sites in an ATM-independent manner, which is different from other forms of oxidative stress such as H2O2 or UV light.\",\"ATR\",\"TP53\",\"\",\"\",\"\"],\"output\":{\"phosphosite\":\"Ser15; Ser37; Ser392\",\"reasoning\":\"The article demonstrates ATR involvement in phosphorylation of p53 at multiple sites under hyperoxia. Specifically, phosphorylation at Ser15, Ser37, and Ser392 decreases when a kinase-inactive ATR is overexpressed, and the authors conclude that hyperoxia activates the ATR-Chk1 pathway to phosphorylate p53 at multiple sites in an ATM-independent manner.\",\"evidence_texts\":[\"Here we demonstrate that hyperoxia phosphorylates the Ser15 residue of p53 independently of ATM.\",\"Additionally, overexpression of kinase-inactive ATM-and-Rad3-related (ATR) in HEK293T cells diminished Ser15, Ser37, and Ser392 phosphorylation compared with vector-only transfected cells.\",\"In contrast, wild-type ATR overexpression did not diminish Ser15, Ser37, or Ser392 phosphorylation.\",\"Together, our data suggest that hyperoxia activates the ATR-Chk1 pathway and phosphorylates p53 at multiple sites in an ATM-independent manner, which is different from other forms of oxidative stress such as H2O2 or UV light.\"],\"confidence\":0.9}},{\"input_row_index\":1,\"row_index\":1,\"input_row\":[\"12959929\",\"Hyperoxia activates the ATR-Chk1 pathway and phosphorylates p53 at multiple sites.\",\"Hyperoxia has been shown to cause DNA damage resulting in growth arrest of cells in p53-dependent, as well as p53-independent, pathways. Although H2O2 and other peroxides have been shown to induce ataxia telangiectasia-mutated (ATM)-dependent p53 phosphorylation in response to DNA damage, the signal transduction mechanisms in response to hyperoxia are currently unknown. Here we demonstrate that hyperoxia phosphorylates the Ser15 residue of p53 independently of ATM. Hyperoxia phosphorylated p53 (Ser15) in DNA-dependent protein kinase null (DNA-PK-/-) cells, indicating 