at Ser37 and Ser392 residues of p53 are also phosphorylated in an ATM-independent manner in hyperoxia. In contrast, H2O2 did not phosphorylate Ser37 in either ATM+/+ or ATM-/- cells. Furthermore, H2O2 failed to phosphorylate Ser15 in ATM-/- cells. Additionally, overexpression of kinase-inactive ATM-and-Rad3-related (ATR) in HEK293T cells diminished Ser15, Ser37, and Ser392 phosphorylation compared with vector-only transfected cells. In contrast, wild-type ATR overexpression did not diminish Ser15, Ser37, or Ser392 phosphorylation. We also show that checkpoint kinase 1 (Chk1) is phosphorylated on Ser345 in response to hyperoxia, which could be inhibited by caffeine or wortmannin, potent inhibitors of phosphoinositide 3-kinase-related kinases. Hyperoxia also phosphorylated Chk1 in ATM+/+ as well as in ATM-/- cells, demonstrating an ATM-independent mechanism in Chk1 phosphorylation. Together, our data suggest that hyperoxia activates the ATR-Chk1 pathway and phosphorylates p53 at multiple sites in an ATM-independent manner, which is different from other forms of oxidative stress such as H2O2 or UV light.\"},\"gt_only\":false,\"source_gt_row_index\":0,\"score_table_row\":1},{\"row_index\":1,\"input_row_index\":1,\"matched\":true,\"run_output\":{\"phosphosite\":\"Ser345\",\"reasoning\":\"The article reports that Chk1 is phosphorylated on Ser345 in response to hyperoxia, and this phosphorylation can be inhibited by caffeine or wortmannin (PIKK inhibitors), consistent with ATR/Chk1 pathway involvement and linking ATR activity to phosphorylation at this residue.\",\"evidence_texts\":[\"We also show that checkpoint kinase 1 (Chk1) is phosphorylated on Ser345 in response to hyperoxia, which could be inhibited by caffeine or wortmannin, potent inhibitors of phosphoinositide 3-kinase-related kinases.\"],\"confidence\":0.85},\"expected\":\"phosphosite: Ser345\",\"ground_truth_output\":{\"phosphosite\":\"Ser345\"},\"comparison_types\":[\"phosphosite: String match\"],\"row_input\":{\"title\":\"Hyperoxia activates the ATR-Chk1 pathway and phosphorylates p53 at multiple sites.\",\"enzyme\":\"ATR\",\"substrate\":\"CHEK1\",\"fulltext\":\"Hyperoxia has been shown to cause DNA damage resulting in growth arrest of cells in p53-dependent, as well as p53-independent, pathways. Although H2O2 and other peroxides have been shown to induce ataxia telangiectasia-mutated (ATM)-dependent p53 phosphorylation in response to DNA damage, the signal transduction mechanisms in response to hyperoxia are currently unknown. Here we demonstrate that hyperoxia phosphorylates the Ser15 residue of p53 independently of ATM. Hyperoxia phosphorylated p53 (Ser15) in DNA-dependent protein kinase null (DNA-PK-/-) cells, indicating that it may not depend on DNA-PK for phosphorylation of p53 (Ser15). We show that Ser37 and Ser392 residues of p53 are also phosphorylated in an ATM-independent manner in hyperoxia. In contrast, H2O2 did not phosphorylate Ser37 in either ATM+/+ or ATM-/- cells. Furthermore, H2O2 failed to phosphorylate Ser15 in ATM-/- cells. Additionally, overexpression of kinase-inactive ATM-and-Rad3-related (ATR) in HEK293T cells diminished Ser15, Ser37, and Ser392 phosphorylation compared with vector-only transfected cells. In contrast, wild-type ATR overexpression did not diminish Ser15, Ser37, or Ser392 phosphorylation. We also show that checkpoint kinase 1 (Chk1) is phosphorylated on Ser345 in response to hyperoxia, which could be inhibited by caffeine or wortmannin, potent inhibitors of phosphoinositide 3-kinase-related kinases. Hyperoxia also phosphorylated Chk1 in ATM+/+ as well as in ATM-/- cells, demonstrating an ATM-independent mechanism in Chk1 phosphorylation. Together, our data suggest that hyperoxia activates the ATR-Chk1 pathway and phosphorylates p53 at multiple sites in an ATM-independent manner, which is different from other forms of oxidative stress such as H2O2 or UV light.\"},\"gt_only\":false,\"source_gt_row_index\":1,\"score_table_row\":2}],\"review_suggestion_seq_next\":1,\"diagnosisComplete\":true,\"stage_log\":[],\"review_method_id\":\"issue_analysis_suggestions\",\"review_stages\":[{\"id\":\"analysis\",\"label\":\"Issue analysis\"},{\"id\":\"suggestions\",\"label\":\"Suggested changes\"}],\"supports_segment_optimization\":true,\"promptText\":\"You are a bioinformatics expert performing literature mining.\\n\\nWe have a **confirmed kinase-substrate pair** from a scientific paper, but the **phosphorylation site was not mentioned in the abstract**. Your job is to find it in the **full text**.\\n\\nArticle title: {title}\\n\\nKinase: {enzyme}\\nSubstrate: {substrate}\\n\\nBelow is the relevant full text of the article.\\n\\n---\\n{fulltext}\\n---\\n\\nIdentify the **specific residue(s) on the substrate that are phosphorylated by the kinase**.\\n\\nReturn only residues on the **given substrate**.\\n\\n## Search Strategy\\n\\n### 1. Direct statements (strongest evidence)\\n\\\"X phosphorylates Y at Ser301\\\", \\\"phosphorylation of Y at Tyr-397 by X\\\", \\\"X adds phosphate to Thr287 of Y\\\", \\\"X-mediated phosphorylation of Y occurs on Ser473\\\".\\n\\n### 2. Site-specific phosphorylation combined with kinase action\\nSometimes the paper states the phosphorylation site on the substrate, and separately that the kinase phosphorylates the substrate or is required for that phosphorylation. In such cases, if the article clearly identifies a specific phosphosite on the substrate and shows that the given kinase is responsible for, required for, or directly causes phosphorylation of that substrate, the phosphosite may be treated as the likely phosphorylation site.\\n\\nDo **not** extract a site merely because it is a known phosphosite of the substrate unless this article links that site to the given kinase.\\n\\n### 3. Experimental evidence identifying a kinase-linked phosphosite\\nSite-specific antibodies (phospho-Tyr397 antibody, pS262 antibody), phosphosite mutants (Y12F, S473A, T308D), phosphopeptides or mass spectrometry, in vitro kinase assays, and phrases describing site positions.\\n\\n## Residue Formats to Recognize\\nFull residue names (Ser301, Thr287, Tyr397), hyphen format (Ser-301, Tyr-397), single-letter format (S301, T287, Y397), phosphorylated notation (pS262, pT217, pY397), mutation notation (Y12F, S473A, T308E), and position descriptions (\\\"position 12\\\", \\\"residue 397\\\").\\n\\n## Extraction Rules\\n\\n1. Extract **only residues on the specified substrate**.\\n2. Prefer **direct statements** assigning phosphorylation of the substrate to the given kinase.\\n3. Extract sites supported by mutants, phospho-antibodies, phosphopeptides, mass spectrometry, or kinase assays only when the evidence links the site to the given kinase.\\n4. If a site is mentioned for the substrate but the article does not connect it to the given kinase, do **not** extract it.\\n5. If multiple sites are reported, return them as a **semicolon-separated string**.\\n6. If no site can be identified, return **null**.\\n7. Use **only information from this article** (no external knowledge).\\n8. In evidence_texts, include the **verbatim sentences** (exact quotes) that mention the site. In reasoning, briefly explain how they support the extraction.\\n\\n## Output Format\\nReturn a valid JSON object with phosphosite, reasoning, evidence_texts (list of verbatim sentences), and confidence (0-1).\",\"workingText\":\"You are a bioinformatics expert performing literature mining.\\n\\nWe have a **confirmed kinase-substrate pair** from a scientific paper, but the **phosphorylation site was not mentioned in the abstract**. Your job is to find it in the **full text**.\\n\\nArticle title: {title}\\n\\nKinase: {enzyme}\\nSubstrate: {substrate}\\n\\nBelow is the relevant full text of the article.\\n\\n---\\n{fulltext}\\n---\\n\\nIdentify the **specific residue(s) on the substrate that are phosphorylated by the kinase**.\\n\\nReturn only residues on the **given substrate**.\\n\\n## Search Strategy\\n\\n### 1. Direct statements (strongest evidence)\\n\\\"X phosphorylates Y at Ser301\\\", \\\"phosphorylation of Y at Tyr-397 by X\\\", \\\"X adds phosphate to Thr287 of Y\\\", \\\"X-mediated phosphorylation of Y occurs on Ser473\\\".\\n\\n### 2. Site-specific phosphorylation combined with kinase action\\nSometimes the paper states the phosphorylation site on the substrate, and separately that the kinase phosphorylates the substrate or is required for that phosphorylation. In such cases, if the article clearly identifies a specific phosphosite on the substrate and shows that the given kinase is responsible for, required for, or directly causes phosphorylation of that substrate, the phosphosite may be treated as the likely phosphorylation site.\\n\\nDo **not** extract a site merely because it is a known phosphosite of the substrate unless this article links that site to the given kinase.\\n\\n### 3. Experimental evidence identifying a kinase-linked phosphosite\\nSite-specific antibodies (phospho-Tyr397 antibody, pS262 antibody), phosphosite mutants (Y12F, S473A, T308D), phosphopeptides or mass spectrometry, in vitro kinase assays, and phrases describing site positions.\\n\\n## Residue Formats to Recognize\\nFull residue names (Ser301, Thr287, Tyr397), hyphen format (Ser-301, Tyr-397), single-letter format (S301, T287, Y397), phosphorylated notation (pS262, pT217, pY397), mutation notation (Y12F, S473A, T308E), and position descriptions (\\\"position 12\\\", \\\"residue 397\\\").\\n\\n## Extraction Rules\\n\\n1. Extract **only residues on the specified substrate**.\\n2. Prefer **direct statements** assigning phosphorylation of the substrate to the given kinase.\\n3. Extract sites supported by mutants, phospho-antibodies, phosphopeptides, mass spectrometry, or kinase assays only when the evidence links the site to the given kinase.\\n4. If a site is mentioned for the substrate but the article does not connect it to the given kinase, do **not** extract it.\\n5. If multiple sites are reported, return them as a **semicolon-separated string**.\\n6. If no site can be identified, return **null**.\\n7. Use **only information from this article** (no external knowledge).\\n8. In evidence_texts, include the **verbatim sentences** (exact quotes) that mention the site. In reasoning, briefly explain how they support the extraction.\\n\\n## Output Format\\nReturn a valid JSON object with phosphosite, reasoning, evidence_texts (list of verbatim sentences), and confidence (0-1).\"}",
